1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
 - Hello, and welcome back to Python as a Language.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:07,460
 You'll notice that I'm wearing a hat.

3
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:11,160
 And part of the story of the hat is that

4
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:13,440
 where I work here at the University of Michigan

5
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:17,360
 School of Information, my office is in this building

6
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:21,540
 called North Quad, and we call it quad-worts sometimes,

7
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:24,780
 because it's sort of got a square,

8
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:27,340
 it sort of imitates an Oxford quad.

9
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:31,440
 And so it seemed to me to evoke notions of Harry Potter,

10
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:33,300
 and when we first moved into the building,

11
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:36,260
 I joked in one of my classes that

12
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:38,720
 we should have a sorting ceremony for all the students

13
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:42,000
 as they come into North Quad for the first time.

14
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,100
 And so that was cool, and I thought that I would belong

15
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:50,700
 in Gryffindor like everyone wants to be in Gryffindor, right?

16
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:51,660
 They're the good guys.

17
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:55,820
 And my students told me that I couldn't be in Gryffindor,

18
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:59,020
 that I had to be in Slytherin.

19
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:01,460
 So you'll see me drinking tea throughout the course

20
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:02,720
 out of this teacup.

21
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:05,400
 It's my Slytherin teacup.

22
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:07,700
 I picked that up from Harry Potter World.

23
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:11,700
 I went down to Florida, and I visited Harry Potter World.

24
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:15,700
 And the reason that I was sorted by my students

25
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:21,860
 into Slytherin is also because I teach Python.

26
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:26,580
 And Python is like a snake.

27
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:31,860
 And so if you think about the people from Slytherin,

28
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:35,160
 they are capable of talking to snakes.

29
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:36,660
 And the class that we were doing the sorting

30
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:40,800
 was a Python class, and so it sort of made perfect sense

31
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,420
 that you would have to be in Slytherin

32
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:45,340
 if you were the Python teacher.

33
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:48,360
 And of course, your name is Charles Severance,

34
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:51,280
 and then that sounds kind of like Severus Snape.

35
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:55,440
 And so I just accepted that I'm in Slytherin, okay?

36
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:00,900
 So you all can be in Gryffindor, but I can't.

37
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:01,740
 I'm in Slytherin.

38
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:04,200
 So I'm the bad guy, or the good guy.

39
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:07,360
 Depends on how you look at it, right?

40
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:10,320
 And so what I'm going to do now is I'm gonna, you know,

41
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:13,240
 bring you into Slytherin as well,

42
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:18,200
 because I'm gonna teach you the Python language.

43
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:22,780
 Python is the language that we Pythonistas talk.

44
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:25,300
 It was invented about over 20 years ago

45
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:30,040
 by a fellow named Guido Van Rossum, and away we go.

46
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:33,060
 Now, even though I'm using this whole snake,

47
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:35,340
 Slytherin thing, it turns out that Python

48
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:37,740
 was not at all named for Harry Potter,

49
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:39,680
 because Python was invented, you know,

50
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:42,800
 almost two decades before Harry Potter was created,

51
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:44,720
 and it wasn't for the snake.

52
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:47,220
 It was actually Monty Python's Flying Circus

53
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:50,900
 as the inspiration for Python, the name Python,

54
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:53,760
 and because Guido Van Rossum really wanted

55
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:55,340
 to create a programming language,

56
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:57,980
 that while it was powerful underneath it

57
00:02:57,980 --> 00:03:00,680
 in its very nature was a very powerful language,

58
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:03,560
 he wanted it to be a language that was fun,

59
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:06,000
 and he wanted it to be a language that was approachable.

60
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,580
 And so that's why Python recently has become

61
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,080
 so absolutely popular,

62
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:17,100
 and it's easy to learn, but it's also powerful,

63
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:18,800
 and that's sort of the magic of Python,

64
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:23,160
 is the ease of learning it, the brevity of the programs,

65
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:27,860
 the shortness of the programs, and the power,

66
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:31,540
 and so we are going to become Pythonistas.

67
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:35,020
 Now, as you learn to be a software developer

68
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:36,960
 using the Python programming language,

69
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:41,080
 you are going to encounter syntax errors,

70
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:44,300
 and I remember when I used to get syntax errors,

71
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:48,320
 and I remember my first programming class,

72
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:51,360
 and I would type on card,

73
00:03:51,360 --> 00:04:04,820
 and I would upload those cards to the computer,

74
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:08,820
 and the computer would say, "You're not worthy."

75
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:11,780
 And I'm like, wait a sec, those are pretty good cards,

76
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:13,620
 how could you be so critical of me?

77
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:17,580
 I'd say syntax error, and I really got sort of

78
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:21,460
 a really bad attitude that somehow

79
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:23,300
 this computer didn't like me,

80
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:26,220
 and that I would make cards, it would complain,

81
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:27,860
 and I would make changes to the cards,

82
00:04:27,860 --> 00:04:28,820
 and it would still complain,

83
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:30,380
 and I'd make changes and it would still complain.

84
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:33,800
 I'm like, how can I win in this situation?

85
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:35,260
 And you're gonna feel the same thing.

86
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:37,260
 You're gonna absolutely feel the same thing.

87
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:39,420
 You're gonna be struggling, you're gonna be like,

88
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:41,180
 how come this computer hates me?

89
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:44,740
 Let me assure you right now, the computer doesn't hate you.

90
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:46,820
 The computer actually loves you.

91
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:50,260
 It just is not very good at showing how it loves you,

92
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:53,540
 or telling you how or why it loves you.

93
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:57,540
 And so syntax errors are not so much

94
00:04:57,540 --> 00:04:59,500
 Python telling you that you're bad,

95
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:01,140
 or that you're an inadequate programmer,

96
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:03,340
 or you should find something else to do.

97
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:05,260
 It's really Python's admission

98
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:07,780
 that it doesn't understand what you're trying to say.

99
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:09,580
 And so you gotta get used to that.

100
00:05:09,580 --> 00:05:11,860
 And it's frustrating, but you gotta get used

101
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:14,180
 to the fact that syntax errors are your friend.

102
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,980
 Python is saying, hey, I got to line seven,

103
00:05:16,980 --> 00:05:18,620
 and I was doing fine up to line seven,

104
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:21,860
 but boy, in line seven, there's some little thing.

105
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:26,100
 I don't know what the word else means in this context,

106
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:28,700
 or you didn't indent it, and so I'm kinda confused.

107
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:29,520
 What did you mean?

108
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:30,940
 Please, please, please help me.

109
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:35,480
 And so it's so much easier for you to learn Python

110
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:38,660
 than it is for Python to figure out what you mean

111
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:40,340
 when you're writing code.

112
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:41,820
 So we have a number of different ways

113
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:45,540
 to sort of encode our instructions when we talk to Python.

114
00:05:45,540 --> 00:05:48,580
 One is we just run Python interactively on our computer.

115
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:50,740
 Hopefully by now you've got it installed,

116
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:53,020
 and you just type Python at a command prompt.

117
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:54,820
 So either a Windows command prompt,

118
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:57,660
 or a Linux command prompt, or a Macintosh command prompt.

119
00:05:57,660 --> 00:05:59,380
 And I got some examples of how to sort of

120
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:02,380
 get this all started, get Python installed,

121
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:03,900
 and away you go.

122
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:05,900
 Now you'll notice when you run the Python interpreter,

123
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:07,540
 the three chevron prompt,

124
00:06:07,540 --> 00:06:10,540
 Python is asking you what next, right?

125
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:13,780
 This is you, it's saying I wanna talk to you,

126
00:06:13,780 --> 00:06:16,620
 I want you to tell me some Python to do.

127
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:17,880
 If you know the Python language,

128
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:19,680
 you know what to say right here.

129
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:23,340
 Now if you know Python, you can type these languages.

130
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:24,820
 You can say, oh, x equals one,

131
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:27,380
 which really means go find a little piece of memory,

132
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:29,420
 label it x, and stick one in it.

133
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:32,100
 Print x is like go find that thing where you labeled it x,

134
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:33,260
 and bring me back that number,

135
00:06:33,260 --> 00:06:34,620
 and tell me what I stored in there.

136
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:35,540
 Now why you wanna do this?

137
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:36,980
 That's a different question.

138
00:06:36,980 --> 00:06:38,500
 And these are very simple things.

139
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:40,260
 It's gonna take you a while to get the big picture

140
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:41,140
 of why we're doing this.

141
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:45,020
 So just trust me that you wanna learn these statements,

142
00:06:45,020 --> 00:06:47,020
 and then later we will successfully

143
00:06:47,020 --> 00:06:49,340
 turn those into a program.

144
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:52,740
 So x equals x plus one, the third line there.

145
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:57,260
 X equals x plus one is not as it seems in math.

146
00:06:57,260 --> 00:06:59,860
 It basically says, hey, go grab the old value of x,

147
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:01,760
 add one to it, and stick it back in x.

148
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:02,600
 That's what that means.

149
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:06,220
 So equal sign really has kind of an arrow to it.

150
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:08,420
 And then we say, hey, go look up that x thing

151
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:10,220
 that we just did, and print that out,

152
00:07:10,220 --> 00:07:12,160
 and then we're gonna say quit.

153
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:14,420
 So that's us talking to Python.

154
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:17,660
 Now, you can type just about any crazy stuff you want in here,

155
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:21,620
 and Python will be unhappy and talk to you.

156
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:25,300
 So what we're gonna do next is we're gonna start talking

157
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:28,060
 about the actual language of Python,

158
00:07:28,060 --> 00:07:30,140
 and what it is that we have to say

159
00:07:30,140 --> 00:07:32,600
 to make Python happy when we're talking to it.

160
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:38,300
 [MUSIC PLAYING]

